WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 310: Henry VII: The Welsh Dragon at Bosworth, the Red and White Rose, and the Dawn of the Tudors

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.080
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series chronicles the ultimate political survivor.

4
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:21.680
[Rebecca] An exile who conquered the realm against all odds, united the rival houses of Lancaster and York, and forged modern England through peace and balance sheets.

5
00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.120
[Rebecca] King Henry V.I.I.

6
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:29.440
[Rebecca] Here is George with the magnificent rise of the Tudors.

7
00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:40.640
[George] Born in January 1457 at Pembroke Castle on the storm-swept coast of Southwest Wales, Henry Tudor was born into an environment of extreme dynastic vulnerability.

8
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:52.560
[George] His father Edmund Tudor, Earl of Richmond, had died of plague in captivity three months before his birth, leaving his 13-year-old mother, Lady Margaret Beaufort, a teenage widow.

9
00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:05.360
[George] Through his mother, Henry carried a tenuous blood claim to the throne descended from John of Gaunt and Catherine Swinford's legitimized Beaufort line, which Henry IV had explicitly barred from royal succession.

10
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:12.800
[George] Raised by his uncle Jasper Tudor, young Henry's childhood was defined by siege and flight during the Wars of the Roses.

11
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:22.320
[George] When the Lancastrian cause was crushed at Tewkesbury in 1471, 14-year-old Henry was the last surviving male of Lancastrian royal blood.

12
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:29.920
[George] Jasper hurried the boy to Tenby Harbour, sailing for France, but a storm blew their vessel onto the coast of Brittany.

13
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.800
[George] For fourteen long years, Duke Francis I.

14
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:42.960
[George] of Brittany held Henry in honorable confinement, treating him as a valuable diplomatic pawn while Yorkist agents constantly attempted to kidnap or extradite him.

15
00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:52.960
[George] This prolonged adolescence in foreign courts cultivated in young Henry an iron self-restraint, extreme secretiveness, and a cold, calculating realism.

16
00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Lady Margaret Beaufort was a woman of formidable intellect, devout piety, and unyielding dynastic purpose.

17
00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:13.720
[George] From her estates in England, she maintained a covert network of spies, couriers, and secret financing to sustain her son in Brittany, orchestrating his political survival through the darkest days of the Yorkist supremacy.

18
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:29.720
[George] In Brittany, young Henry learned the subtle art of courtly dissimulation, mastering French and Latin while listening to the cautious council of European ambassadors, developing a quiet, introspective temperament that rarely revealed emotion or tipped his strategic intentions.

19
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:35.240
[George] Henry's breakthrough came with the mysterious disappearance of the princes in the tower under Richard I.I.I.

20
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:41.240
[George] Disaffected Yorkist exiles led by Edward I.V.'s supporters joined Henry in Brittany.

21
00:02:41.240 --> 00:03:02.920
[George] On Christmas Day 1483, in Rent's Cathedral, twenty-six-year-old Henry Tudor stood before the high altar and swore a historic, solemn oath, if God granted him the English crown, he would marry Princess Elizabeth of York, the eldest daughter and rightful heir of Edward IV, permanently uniting the warring red and white roses.

22
00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:16.040
[George] In August 1485, backed by French subsidies and commanding two thousand French and Scottish soldiers, Henry landed at Mill Bay in Milford Haven, unfurling the ancient red dragon of Cadwilladder.

23
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.920
[George] His march across Midwales rallied thousands of Welsh countrymen who hailed him as Y.

24
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.840
[George] Mab Darogan, the son of prophecy who would deliver Britain.

25
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:34.200
[George] At Bosworth Field on August 22, 1485, despite being inexperienced in warfare, Henry held his nerve as Richard I.i.I.

26
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.960
[George] charged straight toward him.

27
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:48.000
[George] When Lord Stanley's troops intervened to save Henry and Richard was slain, Stanley plucked the battered golden crown from a hawthorn bush and placed it upon Henry's head, proclaiming him King Henry VII.

28
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:55.360
[George] The Christmas Day ceremony at Rentz Cathedral in 1483 was a masterclass in political stagecraft.

29
00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:04.240
[George] By pledging his hand to Elizabeth of York, Henry transformed himself from a narrow Lancastrian partisan into the unifying savior of a divided nation.

30
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:11.040
[George] When he landed in Pembrokeshire in 1485, his small fleet sailed past the craggy headland of St.

31
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:20.240
[George] David's into Milford Haven, stepping ashore at Mill Bay where he dropped to his knees on the sand, kissing the earth and reciting the 43rd Psalm.

32
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.880
[George] Judge me, O God, and plead my cause against an ungodly nation.

33
00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.760
[George] Henry moved with consummate political intelligence to legitimize his new regime.

34
00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:39.280
[George] He dated his reign from the day before the Battle of Bosworth, enabling him to condemn those who fought for Richard as traitors without creating new retroactive offences.

35
00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:50.240
[George] Crowned at Westminster in October 1485, he established the Yeoman of the Guard as his personal royal bodyguard, the oldest continuous military unit in Britain.

36
00:04:50.240 --> 00:05:05.120
[George] In January 1486, he fulfilled his solemn vow, marrying Elizabeth of York in an emotional ceremony that united Lancaster and York, creating the iconic heraldic Tudor Rose, with red petals encircling a white heart.

37
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:15.520
[George] In September 1486, Queen Elizabeth gave birth at Winchester to their firstborn son, whom Henry deliberately named Arthur to evoke the mythical Camelot legend.

38
00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.400
[George] But Yorkist defiance remained fierce.

39
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:31.480
[George] In 1487, Yorkist rebels led by the Earl of Lincoln championed a ten-year-old Oxford organist's son named Lambert Simnell, falsely claiming he was the Earl of Warwick, Edward IV's nephew.

40
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:43.160
[George] Invading from Ireland with two thousand German mercenary pikemen, the rebels clashed with Henry's army at the Battle of Stoke Field in Nottinghamshire on June 16, 1487.

41
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:46.840
[George] It was the true, final clash of the Wars of the Roses.

42
00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:52.200
[George] Lincoln was killed, four thousand rebels were slain, and Simnel was captured.

43
00:05:52.200 --> 00:06:02.440
[George] Demonstrating astute magnanimity, Henry did not execute the boy, but put him to work in the royal kitchens as a turnspit, later promoting him to royal falconer.

44
00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:19.480
[George] The coronation of Elizabeth of York was deliberately delayed until November 1487, after Henry had reigned for two full years and fathered Prince Arthur, ensuring the realm understood he ruled by his own right of conquest and blood, not as a king consort through his wife's title.

45
00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:28.920
[George] The Battle of Stoke Field was so ferocious that the German mercenaries under Martin Schwartz fought to the last man in a sunken lane still known as the Red Gutter.

46
00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:37.960
[George] Simnel's pardoning demonstrated Henry's shrewd understanding that contempt was a far more effective political weapon against pretenders than bloody martyrdom.

47
00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:54.760
[George] A far more dangerous and sophisticated challenge to Henry's crown emerged in 1491 with Perkin Warbeck, a handsome Flemish youth from Tournai who claimed to be Prince Richard, Duke of York, the younger of the two princes in the tower, supposedly spared by assassins.

48
00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:10.360
[George] For eight years, Warbeck was supported by Margaret of Burgundy, sister of Edward IV, Holy Roman Emperor Maximilian I, and King James IV of Scotland, who gave Warbeck a royal cousin in marriage and invaded northern England on his behalf.

49
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:17.520
[George] Warbeck looked remarkably like a plantagenate prince and possessed graceful royal manners that fooled foreign courts.

50
00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:31.040
[George] Henry responded with sophisticated espionage, bribing foreign couriers and uncovering traitors in his own bedchamber, including Sir William Stanley, the very man whose intervention at Bosworth had saved Henry's life.

51
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.280
[George] Stanley was promptly beheaded.

52
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:43.440
[George] In 1497, when Warbeck landed in Cornwall following a massive Cornish tax revolt against Scottish war levies, Henry's forces easily trapped him.

53
00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.480
[George] Captured and paraded through London, Warbeck confessed his true Flemish identity.

54
00:07:48.480 --> 00:08:01.360
[George] After repeated escape attempts from the tower, Warbeck was hanged at Tyburn in 1499 alongside the real, innocent Earl of Warwick, removing the last potential dynastic rival to the Tudor succession.

55
00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:09.360
[George] The international support for Perkin Warbeck demonstrated the lingering European desire to exploit English dynastic instability.

56
00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:18.400
[George] Margaret of Burgundy, living in Maylines, coached the handsome Flemish youth for hours on the private habits and courtly memories of her deceased brother Edward I.

57
00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:22.320
[George] V., providing him with an inner ring of Yorkist exiles.

58
00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:38.240
[George] Henry's counter-espionage network was led by Sir Robert Clifford, who infiltrated Warbeck's inner circle in Flanders, reporting every code and treasonous contact directly to Henry, proving that the pen and the ledger were mightier than the sword in Tudor statecraft.

59
00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.520
[George] Having established dynastic security, Henry V.

60
00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:41.840
[George] I.I.

61
00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:48.800
[George] turned his formidable intellect toward his true passion, financial solvency and the consolidation of royal power.

62
00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:57.680
[George] Remembering how over mighty nobles with private armies had deposed kings throughout the Wars of the Roses, Henry cracked down with unforgiving rigour.

63
00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:10.040
[George] He banned private noble liveries and armed retainers, enforcing compliance through the court of Star Chamber, an elite tribunal of judges that met in Westminster under a ceiling decorated with gilded stars.

64
00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:20.680
[George] To ensure the Crown was financially independent of parliamentary taxation, Henry scrutinized the royal accounts in person, initialing every ledger page with his own hand.

65
00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:25.640
[George] Under his ruthless tax enforcers, Sir Richard Empson and Edmund Dudley.

66
00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:37.560
[George] The Crown extracted astronomical fines from noblemen for ancient forgotten infractions, using what became known as Morton's Fork, an inescapable dilemma devised by Archbishop John Morton.

67
00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.200
[George] If a subject lived extravagantly, he clearly had money to pay the king.

68
00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:47.080
[George] If he lived frugally, he had clearly saved money and could pay just as much.

69
00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:57.560
[George] When Henry died, the English crown possessed nearly two million pounds in gold and silver, transforming England from a bankrupt island into the wealthiest kingdom in Western Europe.

70
00:09:57.560 --> 00:10:13.560
[George] The Court of Star Chamber allowed Henry to bypass corrupt local juries intimidated by powerful neighborhood magnates, hauling great dukes before royal judges in London for intimidating witnesses, maintaining illegal private liveries, or usurping public lands.

71
00:10:13.560 --> 00:10:23.000
[George] When Henry visited his staunch ally the Earl of Oxford at Headingham Castle, Oxford arrayed hundreds of armed retainers in uniform liveries to honor the king.

72
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:32.840
[George] As Henry departed, he coldly informed Oxford, My lord, I thank you for my good cheer, but I may not endure to have my laws broken in my sight.

73
00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:37.960
[George] My attorney must speak with you, fining his loyal commander ten thousand pounds.

74
00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] Henry leveraged his immense wealth to secure prestigious foreign alliances that anchored the Tudor dynasty on the world stage.

75
00:10:45.440 --> 00:11:02.240
[George] In November 1501, he celebrated the crowning diplomatic triumph of his reign, the marriage of his fifteen-year-old heir, Arthur, Prince of Wales, to Princess Catherine of Aragon, daughter of the Catholic monarchs Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of Castile.

76
00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.840
[George] The wedding at St.

77
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:12.240
[George] Paul's cathedral was an explosion of pageantry, with wine flowing from street conduits and Spanish grandees dressed in cloth of gold.

78
00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.760
[George] But triumph turned to ashes barely twenty weeks later.

79
00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:26.160
[George] In April 1502, at Ludlow Castle on the Welsh border, young Prince Arthur died suddenly of the mysterious sweating sickness, leaving the nation in shock.

80
00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:38.480
[George] When the tragic news reached Greenwich, Queen Elizabeth comforted the weeping king, reminding him that God had left them a handsome young prince in ten-year-old Henry, and that they were both young enough to have more children.

81
00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:46.960
[George] But ten months later, in February 1503, Queen Elizabeth died in the Tower of London after giving birth to a short-lived daughter.

82
00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:49.840
[George] Elizabeth's death shattered Henry emotionally.

83
00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:54.800
[George] The king collapsed into profound depression, withdrawing into solitary paranoia.

84
00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:03.920
[George] The Spanish alliance was the diplomatic cornerstone of Henry's foreign policy, codified in the Treaty of Medina del Campo in 1489.

85
00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:17.760
[George] Ferdinand and Isabella, having conquered Granada and united Spain, hesitated to send Catherine to England until all rival plantagenate claimants were eliminated, directly prompting the execution of the young Earl of Warwick.

86
00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:26.080
[George] The sudden death of Arthur at Ludlow cast a dark pall over the alliance, prompting Henry to secure a papal dispensation from Julius I.

87
00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:34.360
[George] I to betroth the young widow Catherine to Arthur's younger brother Henry, preserving the astronomical Spanish dowry of 200,000 ducats.

88
00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:41.160
[George] In his final years, Henry focused his remaining energy on royal architecture and maritime expansion.

89
00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:51.960
[George] He funded the Italian explorer John Cabot's historic voyage of 1497 aboard the Matthew, which discovered Newfoundland and the North American continent for the English crown.

90
00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:59.720
[George] He rebuilt the magnificent royal palace of Sheen on the Thames, renaming it Richmond Palace after his ancestral earldom.

91
00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:16.840
[George] His crowning artistic achievement was the Lady Chapel at Westminster Abbey, begun in 1503, a sublime masterpiece of late perpendicular Gothic featuring dizzying, lace-like fan-vaulted stone ceilings and carved oak choir stalls adorned with brass tudor beasts.

92
00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:24.920
[George] On April 21, 1509, 52-year-old King Henry VIII died of tuberculosis at Richmond Palace.

93
00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:27.960
[George] He had reigned for 23 years and eight months.

94
00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:33.560
[George] Through unyielding discipline, administrative genius, and cold diplomatic realism.

95
00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:48.920
[George] The Welsh exile had resurrected the English monarchy, ended thirty years of fratricidal civil bloodshed, and left an overflowing treasury to his charismatic, athletic seventeen year old son, who would ascend the throne as King Henry VIII.

96
00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:55.720
[George] Henry's legacy was the transformation of medieval feudal England into an early modern bureaucratic state.

97
00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:08.200
[George] He eschewed foreign wars of conquest, knowing they drained the treasury and risked dynastic disaster, preferring lucrative treaties like the Treaty of Etaples, which secured annual French cash pensions.

98
00:14:08.200 --> 00:14:28.160
[George] When Pietro Torrigiano sculpted the magnificent gilt bronze effigies of Henry and Queen Elizabeth for the Westminster Lady Chapel, he captured a king whose face was austere, thoughtful, and worn by vigilance, the king who had quietly, masterfully laid the unshakable foundation of the Tudor Golden Age.

99
00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:32.480
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

100
00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:48.640
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of Henry VII, The Welsh Dragon at Bosworth, The Red and White Rose, and The Dawn of the Tudors, subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

101
00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:51.200
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

